<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The WinUI 3 shell. It is the only desktop application in the solution.

    The window model here was settled by measurement rather than argument (see Shell/HostWindow.cs):
    the top-level window is our own CreateWindowEx HWND, mpv stays a child HWND embedded with its
    "wid" option, and every pixel of chrome lives in a DesktopWindowXamlSource island above the
    video. A real Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Window was tried and rejected: it composites its backdrop into
    the island surface, which makes the island permanently opaque and hides the video child entirely.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.17763.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <AssemblyName>EmbyNian</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>EmbyNian.Shell</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationIcon>..\..\app.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
    <!-- Required by [LibraryImport] source generation in Interop/Native.cs. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <!--
      The language the UI is written in, and the one MRT falls back to. Both halves matter.

      Strings\zh-Hans\Resources.resw is the only resource table this app has, and PRI generation writes
      a default Language qualifier into priconfig.xml — left alone it is en-US, which would make every
      x:Uid lookup ask for a language that has no table and get nothing. DefaultLanguage is what puts
      zh-Hans there instead, so a machine running Windows in English still resolves these strings rather
      than drawing blank labels. NeutralLanguage in Directory.Build.props is the managed-assembly
      counterpart and does not reach the PRI.
    -->
    <DefaultLanguage>zh-Hans</DefaultLanguage>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <!-- Unpackaged: no MSIX, no identity, a plain exe next to libmpv-2.dll. The generated
         bootstrapper resolves the Windows App Runtime at startup, so the SDK version has to be one
         this machine can actually bootstrap. The 1.x line needed a per-release
         MicrosoftCorporationII.WinAppRuntime.Main package, and only 1.4, 1.5 and 1.8 were installed
         (1.6 and 1.7 restored but could not bootstrap). The 2.x line packages its runtime
         differently — one Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.2 framework package, present here at 2.4.0.0 —
         so 2.4.0 bootstraps unpackaged and the startup self-check passes against it. (Spelling that
         switch with its two leading dashes here is what an XML comment cannot hold: MSB4025, and the
         whole project stops loading.) -->
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>
    <WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>false</WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>

    <!-- WinUI 3 cannot run as AnyCPU. The solution maps its Any CPU configuration onto x64 so a
         plain `dotnet build EmbyNian.sln` still builds this project, and Platform is pinned as
         well as Platforms so that building the csproj on its own lands in the same bin\x64 folder
         rather than a second bin\Debug one. -->
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <Platform>x64</Platform>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>

    <!-- We write Main ourselves: the process has to migrate the data directory, take the
         single-instance mutex and start logging before any XAML exists. -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);DISABLE_XAML_GENERATED_MAIN</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    WUI2010 — 「Nested x:Bind path 'ViewModel.EpisodeShelf.Title' will crash if any segment is null at
    startup」 — is the one Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.Analyzers rule this project turns off, and it is off
    here rather than solution-wide so Core and the test project keep the full set. The analyzer is wired
    in by Directory.Build.props; see the comment there for why.

    Twenty sites are flagged, all of them 「shelf leaf」 bindings (DetailPage's four 货架 plus HomePage's
    right rail) and one on PlayerViewModel.SubtitleFont. What the rule cannot see:

    * SubtitleFont is `internal SettingFontRow SubtitleFont { get; }`, assigned in the constructor and
      never null. That one is a plain false positive.
    * The five shelf properties are `CardShelf?` on purpose, and the class comment on
      DetailViewModel.EpisodeShelf says why: a shelf needs the image store and the card width from 设置,
      so it cannot exist before Attach, and the page binds to it from the moment it is constructed. They
      are assigned exactly once, in Attach, and never set back to null.
    * The generated x:Bind code null-checks every segment of a path before descending, so a null
      intermediate leaves the target unset — it does not throw. 「will crash」 overstates it.

    Both fixes the rule offers cost more than they buy here, which is why this is a suppression rather
    than a change:

    * A flat view-model property (`EpisodeTitle => EpisodeShelf?.Title ?? ""`) goes stale the moment the
      shelf's own Title changes, because the outer object raises nothing for an inner change. Making it
      correct means subscribing to each shelf's PropertyChanged and re-raising — fifteen properties plus
      a subscription ring, to replace a binding the framework already keeps current.
    * A non-null `CardShelf.Empty` placeholder would report a wrong RowHeight and a true
      RailVisibility before the real shelf arrives, i.e. it converts 「not there yet」 into 「there but
      wrong」. That is the 「界面骗人」 failure this codebase has paid for twice.

    What would make this suppression wrong: any code path that sets one of those five properties back to
    null after Attach, or a sixth nested x:Bind path added over a property that really can go null both
    ways. Neither exists today — grep for the five names in ViewModels/DetailViewModel.cs and
    ViewModels/HomeViewModel.cs before adding one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);WUI2010</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The parts of the Windows App SDK this app uses, rather than the Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK
      meta-package. That package is ten sub-packages in a trench coat, and four of them are for
      features a video client does not have: .ML pulls in Microsoft.Windows.AI.MachineLearning, whose
      onnxruntime.dll (21 MB) and DirectML.dll (18 MB) were 39 MB of inference runtime shipped beside
      a player that runs no inference, and .Search (which drags .AI along), .AI and .Widgets add
      several more. Nothing in this repository names Microsoft.Windows.AI, .Search or .Widgets, and
      the meta-package contributes nothing else — its props and targets only declare a VS project
      capability.

      The versions are exactly what Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK 2.4.0's nuspec resolves to, so the
      framework package the bootstrapper finds (Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.2 2.4.0.0 on this
      machine) is unchanged: same runtime, fewer files copied. Going back is one line — put the
      meta-package reference back and delete these six.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.Base" Version="2.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.Foundation" Version="2.3.9" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.InteractiveExperiences" Version="2.1.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.WinUI" Version="2.3.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.DWrite" Version="2.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.Runtime" Version="[2.4.0]" />
    <!-- MVVM, source-generated. The pages bind to view models rather than reaching for named XAML
         elements, and this package is what makes that cheap enough to be worth doing everywhere:
         [ObservableProperty] on a partial property writes the backing field, the setter and the
         change notification, and [NotifyPropertyChangedFor] wires a derived property to its source
         so a Visibility that follows a bool cannot go stale. Hand-written INotifyPropertyChanged is
         what CardItem does — 40 lines of Raise() calls that a reviewer has to check by eye — and
         doing that eight more times, once per page, is how the notifications start being wrong. -->
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.2" />
    <!-- The container the services are registered in. It replaces a composition root that built the
         same objects by hand, and the reason to prefer it is ValidateOnBuild: an object that cannot be
         constructed is now a startup failure naming the missing dependency, where the hand-written
         version could only fail later, as a NullReferenceException on whichever page happened to touch
         it first. Registered in Composition/ShellServices.cs; the container itself stays in this
         project so Core keeps taking no package references and the test runner can still reach the
         services without one. Same major as the framework, for the reason given just below. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- DPAPI, for the saved password and token. WinUI does not reference the desktop framework, so
         this is an explicit package. Kept on the same major as the target framework: an 8.0.x here
         drags the 8.0 System.Runtime contract into a net10.0 build for no reason. -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\EmbyNian.Core\EmbyNian.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The player is libmpv loaded in-process, so the DLL has to sit next to the exe in every
       configuration and not only in a publish. It is 117 MB; PreserveNewest means it is copied once and
       then skipped, and the Condition means a checkout without it still builds and simply reports the
       missing backend at play time instead of failing the build. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\libmpv-2.dll" Condition="Exists('..\..\libmpv-2.dll')">
      <Link>libmpv-2.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The shader files the 着色器档位表 names, from assets/shaders in this repository.

    Two things changed here on 2026-09-03. They used to be copied out of the user's own portable mpv
    installation, at a hard-coded C:\mpv_config-2026.08.12 — which meant this program could only be published
    correctly on a machine where another program happened to be installed, and the ten files were listed a
    second time here because MSBuild cannot read the C# catalogue. And they used to be copied only
    AfterTargets="Publish", so an ordinary build produced an exe with no shaders next to it and the self-check
    reported every file missing.

    Now they are in the repository and this is a plain None/CopyToOutputDirectory item, so a build and a
    publish both get them, and the wildcard cannot drift from the catalogue the way a second hand-written
    list could. What is still worth guarding is the other direction — a chain naming a file that is not in
    assets/shaders, or a file in assets/shaders that no chain names — and that is one unit test
    (「着色器档位：装箱的文件和档位表点名的一字不差」) reading the same directory.

    .md is excluded: assets/shaders/README.md records where each file came from and is not for shipping.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\assets\shaders\**\*.glsl;..\..\assets\shaders\**\*.hook">
      <Link>shaders\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    libmpv's one non-system dependency, from assets/mpv-runtime in this repository.

    It used to be copied out of the user's own portable mpv installation after a publish — every dll in
    that folder's root, which was vulkan-1.dll and a lua51.dll libmpv never loads. Same objection as the
    shaders above: whether this program could be published, and whether the published copy could open a
    video at all, depended on another program still being installed at a hard-coded path.

    Shipping it is not optional and not 「only when the user picks Vulkan」: vulkan-1.dll is a *static*
    import of libmpv-2.dll (that dll has no delay-load table), so libmpv fails to load without it, and
    gpu-api defaults to vulkan anyway. The system copy cannot be relied on — it arrives with a GPU driver.
    assets/mpv-runtime/README.md records the provenance and how to re-check the import table after an
    upgrade. .md is excluded for the same reason as the shaders.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\assets\mpv-runtime\*.dll">
      <Link>%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The bundled 字幕字体, from assets/fonts in this repository — 「这个字体打包进程序里」（2026-09-06）.
    The shipped 方正中等线简体 is the 装机默认 of 设置 → 字幕 → 字体, and mpv is pointed at this folder by
    sub-fonts-dir at every launch (ShellServices → PlaybackPlanner → MpvBaseline), so it renders without
    being installed into Windows. The folder is also part of what FontLibrary scans, so the picker offers
    it with a real preview on a machine that does not have it.

    Kept next to the shaders and vulkan-1.dll rather than as ms-appx resources: playback runs through
    libmpv on a plain path, not through a package URI.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\assets\fonts\*.ttf;..\..\assets\fonts\*.otf;..\..\assets\fonts\*.ttc">
      <Link>fonts\%(Filename)%(Extension)</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>


</Project>
